A||N||12||FieroHarry||3.4 Liter swap ?||||6||
Z||000000||FieroHarry||10-15-2001||08:02 PM||||Okay Ed, Archie, somebody with info......<P>I got offered a freshly rebuilt 3.4 from a late model Firebird today.<BR>ECM/harness/engine, the works for $500.... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Is this a viable swap option?<P>Will it bolt right up to the 4 speed?<P>Can i use my intake (this one points in wrong direction) its a rear wheel drive car..<P>I know about starter switching from one side to the other......<P>Ever since driving Ed Parks 3.1 swap last year ive wanted to do something like this,<P>Could I, should I, try this or would it be better to wait till I find a good 3.1?<P>Any and all info would be greatly appreciated!<P><P>------------------<BR><BR><B>"If you want to blend in, take the bus!"</B>||||reg||6
Z||000001||mrfiero||10-15-2001||08:16 PM||||Ed Parks has been doing 3.4 swaps for years now.....I think that is the engine you drove last year (not a 3.1).<P>The Camarao/Firebird 3.4 is essentially the same as the Fiero 2.8.  They are dimensionally the same, but they require some work.  The biggets is the starter relocation, which you know about.  <P>You can (and have to) use your Fieros intake manifolds, exhaust manifolds, distributor, wiring harness, ECM, fuel injection, etc.  When you are done it will look like a stock 2.8.<P>There is a ton of info on this swap......just go to the archives and search 3.4 swaps.<P> <P>  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroHarry||10-15-2001||08:39 PM||||Thanks mrfiero,<BR>I was doing just that when you replied to this. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||10-15-2001||08:44 PM||||It's possible to use the Camaro ECM with the fiero intake manifold. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||10-15-2001||08:47 PM||||With some creative welding, you can also use the Camaro intake  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Sootah||10-15-2001||09:00 PM||||Where would the best 3.4 swap site be? I can pretty much just change my oil, and am sure I could give a good ole fashioned tune up with ease. How long does the engine take to drop, what is the easiest 3.4 block to use and how hard is the starter crap to relocate? Also, why can't you just use the Camaro/Firebird starter and leave it in the stock location? Dumb question prolly but I really want to know. Ah, yes, how much should a good short block cost me?<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Nuclear_Happy_Face_Sig.jpg">     <BR><sup><I>Never pull your pants down in Cosco, the dang monkeys will getcha</I></sup> <BR><sup><B>AIM</B>: KSSouter</sup>||||reg||5
Z||000006||mrfiero||10-16-2001||02:25 AM||||If you go the RWD 3.4 route then you will definitely need to relocate the starter.  On RWD applications the starter is on the opposite side of the block (compared to FWD).....the starter interferes with the axles and therefore can't be left in its original location.  <P>You must drill the new starter holes very carefully or the starter will not line up properly.  I would suggest buying  the 3.4 starter template (I think someone had one for sale in the Mall) or having a professional do the work for you.<P>As far as how long it takes to drop an engine......it depends on how many issues you run into along the way.  Some rust belt cars have problems with the cradle bolts seizing up, so you could conceiveably spend a few hours just getting the bolts out.  Typically though it should only take a few hours.  There are a lot of things that must be disconnected, so make sure you have everything loose that needs to be loosened before lowering the cradle.<P>I have yet to be able to find just a shortblock for the 3.4......all of the junkyards around here want to sell complete engines.  The ones that do sell shortblocks don't have any 3.4's.  If they did I could get one for $36!  Plan on spending $300-$700 (or more) for a complete 3.4 engine or $100-$300 or so for a shortblock (if you can find one).<P>I lucked out on mine......I got a 3.4 builder that is already tapped and drilled for the Fiero for $250.  I just need to rebuild it and throw it in the GT.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Since you don't have a lot of experience  with major repairs you might not want to undertake this by yourself.....it could cause a lot of frustration and loss of interest in Fieros (or cars in general).  It might actually be cheaper to just buy another Fiero and either sell this one as is (even at a loss) or part it out.<P>Food for thought, anyway.<P>Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000007||revin||10-16-2001||07:30 AM||||I was told by the local engine rebuilder here that I caoul trade in my 2.8 block for a 3.1 block. OR stuff a 3.1 crank in the 2.8 for $800 ! Very tempting! Add a little cam, and a little bore job and voli!!!! sounds better than messing with all the starter stuff.what do ya think?||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfixit58||10-16-2001||07:59 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by revin:<BR><B>I was told by the local engine rebuilder here that I could trade in my 2.8 block for a 3.1 block. OR stuff a 3.1 crank in the 2.8 for $800 ! Very tempting! Add a little cam, and a little bore job and voli!!!! sounds better than messing with all the starter stuff.what do ya think?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I think the starter stuff is easier if you have a good 3.4 short block / long block. If you gotta rebuild the 3.4 then it's a toss up. If you have to go out a buy a 3.4 to rebuild the the 3.1 is probably cheaper and easier. Just remember the saying... "There's no substitute for cubic inches".  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Roy  :D <BR>Just another stock Blue 87 GT   <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG">      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif"> ||||reg||6
Z||000009||mshill||10-16-2001||12:43 PM||||I'm with mrfixit, it only takes about an hour to drill and tap the starter holes on the other side of a 3.4 RWD block if you have the drill guide.||||reg||1
